WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.340
Hello everyone.  So today we are going to talk about Zika virus.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.680
We are going talk about mosquitoes.  We're going to talk about pregnancy.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:16.760
We are also going to focus on what you, the high school student, can do and should be doing to protect yourself from mosquito bites.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:22.140
So I want to start today and talk
about the World Health Organization

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.900
and you'll see that WHO stands for the
World Health Organization.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:31.360
And the World Health Organisation announced, on the first of February this year,

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.380
a public health emergency of international concern.  We've only ever had four,

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:43.920
and this was the fourth.  Our first one was in April 2009 when we had pandemic influenza or pandemic flu.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.000
In May of 2014 we had another emergency for polio.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.640
In August of 2014, we had an emergency for Ebola.  And I had three trips to West Africa

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:00.539
setting up Ebola hospitals and treating Ebola patients.  And then on the 1st of February

00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:06.339
this year, in 2016, we had another public
health emergency.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:12.860
And this wasn't because of the mosquitoes, this wasn't related to the virus, this is because in Brazil

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.960
they were seeing babies born with what's
called microcephaly,

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.780
or their heads were much smaller than they should be.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.979
And then something amazing happened because

00:01:23.979 --> 00:01:27.110
when this announcement, when this announcement  and this emergency happened  on the first of February,

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:31.320
we didn't know a lot about the Zika virus.  We didn't know about, a lot about,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.680
the Zika virus and the effect it would
have on pregnant women,

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:41.320
let alone the effect it would have on unborn babies. And on April 13th, only a few weeks ago

00:01:41.329 --> 00:01:46.759
this year, a new study was published by
colleagues of mine from the Center for

00:01:46.759 --> 00:01:51.750
Disease Control in Atlanta, Georgia.  And
here's a copy of this paper that's freely

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:56.319
available on the internet for you to
read.  And they published the first

00:01:56.329 --> 00:02:02.399
evidence that Zika virus was related to birth defects and how it affected pregnant women.

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:10.940
So we now know today, we now know that
the Zika virus infects, can infect

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.310
pregnant women, and can somehow

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:19.099
get into those babies and cause birth
defects.  And the birth, the main birth defect

00:02:19.099 --> 00:02:23.459
we're seeing is called microcephaly.  And
this slide I want to show you what a

00:02:23.459 --> 00:02:29.459
typical head size of a baby looks like.  A
baby with microcephaly has a head size

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:34.630
that's smaller than normal and a baby
with severe microcephaly has a much very

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:39.790
much smaller head size.  Now why do they
have smaller head sizes?  Because we now know

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:47.530
that the Zika virus attacks the brain and
the Zika virus affects the growth and development of the brain.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.860
And babies fetuses that are in the mother, in the womb,

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:56.600
before they are  born, as the brain grows the head grows.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:03:01.730
Now if you have a virus like Zika that
attacks the brain, the head doesn't grow

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:06.640
as fast and that's why we're getting
babies born with smaller head sizes.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:10.080
But we're also seeing other problems at the
moment.  We will see seeing babies that

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.000
are born, and these very newborn babies, these young infants, we are seeing

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.180
problems with their eyes, with their sight.  We are seeing problems with their hearing.  We are seeing

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:22.150
problems with their growth and
development.  And so there's still a lot

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:24.650
we need to learn about the effects of
Zika virus

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:30.990
and the effects of that has on pregnant women, on fetuses, and on newborn babies.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:38.799
And the way that we measure this is we take a tape measure when the baby's born.  So we wait 24 hours

00:03:38.799 --> 00:03:42.359
after the baby's born, we put a tape
measure around their head,

00:03:42.359 --> 00:03:44.340
and we measure the size of their head.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.320
But it's really important that when we worked with the nurses,

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:53.120
with the doctors, with the community health workers around the world that I work with

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.000
that they all take the same measurements.
So we are doing lots of course and lots of

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.880
training at the moment to teach nurses
and doctors and community health workers

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.880
how to take this measurement.  And we can
see that, by putting a tape measure

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.510
around the head, whether the head is normal or below normal.  And then we'll repeat

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:13.639
this every week, and will repeat this for
a number of years, to look at the

00:04:13.639 --> 00:04:16.329
development of the baby.  And this is
really important.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:20.910
Another way that we can diagnose what's
called microcephaly before the baby

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.360
is born is by taking ultrasounds.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.780
Now the the emergency we have so far

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.849
is so large that we don't have time to
go and teach everyone in the world how

00:04:31.849 --> 00:04:36.539
to read these ultrasounds.  So what we do,
we get them to take an ultrasound of the

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:40.120
pregnant woman, and then we use
telemedicine like we doing today.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:45.400
The same video conferencing software that
you are using today we use for them to show

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.300
us what the ultrasound of the pregnant
woman's baby looks like.  And then we get

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:54.479
other physicians and nurses who are much
better, much more expert in the field of

00:04:54.479 --> 00:04:59.319
ultrasound, and they help them diagnose
whether the baby has a small head.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.360
Since that emergency's been announced the first of February I've been on TV a number of times.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:12.560
I've been on CNN. I've been on the BBC. I've been on Canada CTV. I've been on Al-jazeera.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:18.340
And the challenge that I face as a public health expert is, that like this one here on Al-jazeera,

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.920
Zika virus fears. More Americans test positive after traveling.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:26.860
What I want to tell you today there's nothing to be scared about.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.900
And we have to ensure that people are
educated, they're aware, and they

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:35.779
understand what the Zika virus is, how
it's transmitted by mosquitoes, how do we

00:05:35.779 --> 00:05:39.900
protect ourselves, and what we need to do
to decrease that risk.  And it's so

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:43.870
important that we not scared about this
but we were actually we take action, and we

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:47.630
do the right thing to protect us from
being bitten by mosquitoes.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:55.240
And here is a map of Brazil, and this is where it all started about May 2015.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:02.160
We've known about the Zika virus since 1947.  It comes from a very beautiful forests in the country of

00:06:02.169 --> 00:06:07.440
Uganda, in Africa.  And it really hasn't
been related to causing birth defects in

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.140
babies for many - until very recently -
about 12 months ago.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:18.479
And it started in Brazil and here, in Brazil, you can see the red dots, and the red dots show where

00:06:18.479 --> 00:06:25.599
these babies that are being born with
small heads actually started being reported in 2015;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.780
and that's why the World Health Organization in Februaryof this year announced the  emergency.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:37.180
And what we've seen, very rapidly, very quickly, the

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:39.760
spread of the Zika virus,

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:44.300
not just as we've seen it before in
Africa and Asia; but when it appeared in

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:48.680
Brazil, it spread all through other
countries in South America.  It went to

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:53.259
Central America, it went to the Caribbean,
it's now in the Pacific Islands.  It's

00:06:53.259 --> 00:06:56.910
been there before, but it's going back
there now.  It's in Hawaii, for example.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:07:00.569
We've had met people that have traveled
to Samoan people will travel to Brazil

00:07:00.569 --> 00:07:04.940
and come back to Hawaii.  We don't see local transmission so it's not in the mosquito

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:09.300
population, but it's starting to appear in
many countries throughout the world.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:14.270
And we're at nearly sixty countries right
now where the mosquitoes are transmitting

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:19.289
Zika virus, but not here in the United
States.  The cases that we've had here in

00:07:19.289 --> 00:07:22.970
the United States have just been from
people traveling to any of these

00:07:22.979 --> 00:07:25.419
countries here that are colored in blue.

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.060
So if we look at the information that we have

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:34.940
today here in the United States, the
Centers for Disease Control Atlanta

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:39.590
tells us that we have laboratory confirmed these are people that have been sick, gone to a

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:43.789
doctor, had a blood sample, and the blood
samples being sent to a laboratory and

00:07:43.789 --> 00:07:47.750
have tested positive for the Zika virus, we have 388

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:51.330
cases.  Some of those have been in
pregnant women.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:53.960
All of those cases, all of the

00:07:53.979 --> 00:07:59.659
388, have traveled outside the US to
countries with known Zika virus.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:03.860
In the US territories is a little bit different;
the story is a little bit different.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:09.990
We've had 503 known laboratory 
confirmed cases - America Samoa,

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:15.310
Puerto Rico, and the US Virgin Islands.  Now
those three countries are different

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:20.659
because the virus is now in their mosquito
populations, and it's possible, if you

00:08:20.659 --> 00:08:25.919
live in Porto Rico, the US Virgin Islands, or
American Samoa, if you're bitten by

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:28.990
a mosquito, you may be able to get this
Zika virus.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:35.780
And we're really concerned about two types of mosquitoes,

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:39.600
and these two types of mosquitoes are really easy to identify.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:45.680
The first one is the Aedes
aegypti mosquito and it really is it's not

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.680
native here in the US.  It actually
came from Africa, but it has been here, it

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.360
doesn't live here now in the US, and we
sometimes

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:54.420
call this the yellow fever mosquito.

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:58.060
The other mosquito we are worried about is the Aedes

00:08:58.089 --> 00:09:02.589
albopictus which comes from Asia, and it
now lives here in the United States, and

00:09:02.589 --> 00:09:04.809
we call that the Asian tiger mosquito.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:07.700
And these mosquitoes in your backyard, in

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:13.240
your home, where you live, are really easy
to identify.  Have a look closely at these

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.180
two photos.  Can you see their legs?

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:17.820
Can you see the white stripes?

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:25.360
Aides species mosquito have white stripes on their legs.  So if you're at your house your home and

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:28.610
you see those mosquitos and they don't
have any stripes,

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:33.740
then they're not the Aedes type.  But if they do have white stripes, then they belong to

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:38.180
Aides species of mosquito, and they're the
mosquitoes we are worried about.

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:46.060
And the Center for Disease Control, the CDC, in Atlanta, Georgia has produced two maps -

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:51.440
and here's the first map on the left
is the Aedes aegypti, that first mosquito

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.029
we looked at, and these are the states in
the middle of summer where these

00:09:55.029 --> 00:10:01.510
mosquitoes have been found in previous
years.  The Aedes albopictus mosquito is

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:04.829
shown on the map on the right hand side
and you can see the states where they,

00:10:05.399 --> 00:10:11.199
we know they live.  But that doesn't show us
the risk.  And what it shows is here is

00:10:11.199 --> 00:10:15.209
that there's going to be about 30 states in
the US this year that are going to have

00:10:15.209 --> 00:10:21.040
the right mosquitoes to spread the virus.
But they may not spread the virus and

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:22.240
I'm going to show you why.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:26.720
So first of all let's look at and talk a little bit about risk.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:32.540
If we go back to this these, these maps that are on the CDC website, it doesn't really

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:37.010
tell us is a mosquito in my backyard?
Does it live?  Because the mosquito

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:42.420
doesn't live at high altitudes.  It lives
at altitudes down where it is warm and it's hot,

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:48.449
it's wet.  And it doesn't live at areas where above 6,500 feet and

00:10:48.449 --> 00:10:50.329
it's not shown on this map.

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:55.459
A study that came out two days ago by NASA actually

00:10:55.459 --> 00:10:59.519
took all the information we've had from
previous years and started to generate

00:10:59.519 --> 00:11:04.930
maps based on risk by month.  And let's
look here, the Aedes aegypti mosquito,

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:08.820
and we know in winter it's cold and
mosquitoe is not around.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:13.520
But we know that in May, June, July, August, and September, can you see those dots?

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:20.940
The red, the orange dot is high, the yellow dot is low risk and shows with these mosquitoes have been

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:25.140
found and where we're going to see risk.  But it doesn't take us down to a local level

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:29.740
and what's really interesting is that,
even though we may have the mosquito

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:33.010
present, it doesn't mean it's going to
transmit the virus.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.140
And this is why.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:43.700
Because in the US, the only way that
mosquito can get infected is if the

00:11:43.709 --> 00:11:48.540
mosquito bites someone that already has
the infection, and that's a really

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:52.510
important message to understand.  You
got to understand that for the mosquito to

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:56.899
get infected, it has to bite someone that
has the infection, and they have the virus

00:11:56.899 --> 00:12:00.739
in the blood.  And we all know that
mosquitoes, when they bite us, they are sucking blood.

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:06.399
These mosquitoes, the Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus, can also

00:12:06.399 --> 00:12:10.580
transmit other viruses.  And you may have
heard of diseases like yellow fever.

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:15.529
We give a vaccination for that.  You may have heard of diseases like dengue.  There's been a

00:12:15.529 --> 00:12:20.230
dengue outbreak for the last six months
in Hawaii.  You may have heard of diseases

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:23.950
like chikungunya, which is a new disease
for the last couple of years, and again

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:29.130
that's spread by the same mosquito. Zika is
spread by these mosquitoes.  And so this

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:35.010
mosquito has to bite someone that is infected before can transmit the virus to someone else,

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:42.780
and this is how it does it.  The
mosquito bites a person with the infection.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:47.600
These mosquitoes mainly bite in the day so
we were really, really looking for

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:51.339
mosquitoes that bite in the day, but don't be
fooled by that.  These mosquitoes can also

00:12:51.339 --> 00:12:56.160
bite at night.  We get these mosquitoes
here in Washington, DC, and I know last

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:59.820
year I got bitten when I was out having
a BBQ with my family.  We get bitten even

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:04.560
when it's dark, so they're not just
daytime biters.  When they bite you, they

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:09.399
suck in the blood that takes in the
virus, the Zika virus.  It goes down to

00:13:09.399 --> 00:13:13.630
their stomach, their gut.  It then enters
their bloodstream, their circuitry system,

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:18.410
and it goes back up to their salivary
glands, which is in their mouth part

00:13:18.410 --> 00:13:23.569
their proboscis.  Then, when they take
the next bite, when they inject saliva,

00:13:23.569 --> 00:13:29.669
that's where the virus is.  This cycle
in the mosquito takes about 10 days.

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:35.120
So even if a mosquito bites an infected person, it's about 10 days before that mosquito

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:41.280
can transmit the virus.  Which of these
mosquitoes actually bite?  Guess what?

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:46.819
The males don't bite; the boy mosquitoes
don't bite.  It's the female mosquitoes.

00:13:46.819 --> 00:13:51.629
The female mosquitoes are the ones that
bite us.  And it's really important now

00:13:51.629 --> 00:13:55.399
that what we are doing in communities - and I've been doing a lot of work with high

00:13:55.399 --> 00:13:59.779
schools in South America and Central
America, even here in the US, in states

00:13:59.779 --> 00:14:04.569
like Georgia, Alabama, and Tennessee and
Florida - we are getting students now to

00:14:04.569 --> 00:14:09.180
actually help us identify where these
mosquitoes with white bands on their legs

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:13.620
are actually there.  Are they in your
neighborhood?  And the reason we doing this

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:17.800
is, as we know that these mosquitoes
don't fly very far.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:25.100
They fly about the length of a football field, about a hundred and fifty feet.  So if you find

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:29.889
mosquitoes with white legs in your
backyard, they fly about the length of a

00:14:29.889 --> 00:14:33.760
football field, and then they'll come back
in the evening and they really stay locally.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:39.980
And so we can control these mosquitoes locally, then we can decrease the risk of Zika virus.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:45.519
This slide here shows what's been

00:14:45.519 --> 00:14:49.959
happening a lot in medicine.  Public
health hospitals have been using this for years.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:55.420
We reproduce what's called infographics and this is an infographic produced by the

00:14:55.430 --> 00:15:00.810
CDC which focuses on not just Zika, but
chikungunya, and dengue.

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:05.020
And here this infographic is explaining what local transmission.

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.920
Remember we talked about the mosquitoes in Puerto Rico,

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:11.440
American Samoa, the US Virgin Islands.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:15.980
Local transmission is occurring in those three countries.  Local transmissions not

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:24.550
occurring in the US 48 states, also Hawaii
and Alaska.  So here where local

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:28.459
transmission, you gotta understand this term, is where mosquito bites a person who's

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:33.440
infected again we know takes about 10 days for the bars to go through the mosquito,

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:36.320
then the mosquito then bites someone who's healthy

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:38.800
and they inject the virus in the saliva.

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:42.980
Now within three to seven days that person may become sick.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:47.160
But we  know with Zika virus not everyone's going to become sick,

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:48.640
only some people will.

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:56.980
What we're having here in the United States right now is what's called an imported case of Zika, and these

00:15:56.980 --> 00:16:01.890
important cases where someone goes on
for business trip, goes on vacation, down

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:06.820
to those countries I showed on the map that are blue, they get bitten by a mosquito that

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:12.280
has the Zika virus.  Within three to seven
days they may feel sick, some may feel sick,

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:16.840
and they come back to the US and
we diagnose them through a laboratory

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:20.470
tests they have the virus, and that's called,
that's a imported case, and that's what

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:24.610
we're seeing now the United States.  What
we're concerned about is getting local

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:30.560
transmission, as we get more of these
mosquitoes in spring and summer,

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:36.600
and the potential for getting more people infected with this Zika virus leading to local transmission.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:41.900
And when we talked about people getting sick from the Zika virus,

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:45.700
they really have four symptoms we're going to focus on.  They get a fever.

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:50.600
They get a rash.  They could have joint pain, but not always.

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:55.490
They could have red eyes, which we call
conjunctivitis, and it's very similar to

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:59.010
these other diseases that are transmitted by mosquitoes called dengue and chikungunya.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.140
What makes these three
diseases unique here in the United

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:08.650
States is these are nationally
notifiable diseases or national notifiable conditions.

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:13.200
So that if you're sick, and
you go to see your physician, your doctor,

00:17:13.209 --> 00:17:18.849
or nurse, and they test you and that test
comes back positive, by law,

00:17:18.849 --> 00:17:24.100
by legislation, they have to report this
disease to the local county health

00:17:24.110 --> 00:17:27.910
department, who then reports them to the
state health department, who then reports it

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:32.510
to the Center for Disease Control Atlanta.  And that's what we mean by nationally notifiable diseases.

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:36.620
And I just recently come back from South America.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:40.040
I went to five countries. I saw a
lot of people that were sick

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:44.280
and i can tell you now that not everyone had all these four symptoms.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:48.460
Nearly everyone had fever, but not
everyone had rash.

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:54.200
Nearly everyone had fever, and some had red eyes or conjunctivitis,

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:58.060
but not everyone.  So this is still a very challenging disease for us to diagnose.

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:02.880
When you look at what do we do.  How do we protect ourselves?

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:07.140
Now you could be sick Zika.  You could be sick with chikungunya.  You could be sick with dengue.

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:11.080
But it's really important that we have to
take responsibility for ourselves and

00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:15.000
protect ourselves.  And it's really
important that, if we have these symptoms,

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:19.410
that over that seven-day period where we
may be able to transmit the virus to

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:24.110
mosquitoes, we have to prevent ourselves
from being bitten by mosquitoes.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:31.820
And the ways that we can do that - well, we know that daytime is the most likely time for

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:36.260
these mosquitoes to bite.  These mosquitoes with white bands on their legs

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:40.900
most  likely bite in the day time, especially when the sun comes up  at dawn

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:43.960
and when the sun goes in the dust.  But
remember, it's not always the case,

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:46.430
they can bite at the night time, so we got to  protect ourselves.

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:54.850
We might want to protectourselves with clothing - socks, long pair of trousers, shirts, long shirts - from being bitten.

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:59.220
We want to use insect repellent.
And so one of the questions that I get

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:02.540
asked, I teach at both Harvard University
and Penn State University, when all our

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:06.130
students were going on spring break, one
of the questions that all the students

00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:10.640
asked me was, "Hey we're going on spring
break.  Do we put our sunscreen on first

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:12.940
or our insect repellent on first?"

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:17.260
And it's a really good question, and you put the sunscreen on first and then you put the

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:21.260
insect repellent on second, and not many
people know that, but that's the way you do it.

00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:25.560
Again we can wear protective
clothing, but we can also, what we can do

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:30.820
as individuals, within our families, we
can mosquito proof our homes.

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:36.580
We can decrease the likelihood of the
mosquitoes laying their eggs, reproducing

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:40.990
around our homes, and we can decrease the risk of being bitten by a  mosquito.

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:47.930
So what the Center for Disease Control -
New York has done this, Florida has done this,

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:54.790
Alabama, California, Louisiana, Hawaii's
doing this - they putting together the

00:19:54.790 --> 00:20:00.320
prevention kits.  And the work that I've
been doing in high schools is actually

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:04.570
helping them raise some money.  We know
that we can put these kits together.

00:20:04.570 --> 00:20:09.400
They have a bed net.  They have these little larvae site tablets where we can put them in water and

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:18.210
it kills the mosquito larvae.  We have
some deet, or some insect repellent that

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:22.630
it is known to work, and there is a list of those we can provide.  We used the Methrin spray

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:27.050
on our clothes.  We don't put it on our skin.
We put it on our clothes.  We know that we can

00:20:27.050 --> 00:20:31.370
take out temperature in case we have a
fever.  We now know that Zika can be

00:20:31.370 --> 00:20:37.650
transmitted sexually from men to women through semen, so we can provide

00:20:37.650 --> 00:20:41.260
condoms.  And it's really interesting, because
high school students that I've been working  with

00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:45.320
with have been putting through these kits
and selling them to people in their

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:49.360
community - one, to raise the awareness of
mosquitoes and the potential getting

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:53.970
Zika, but also to make some money.  And I
had some students knock on my door in

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:58.350
Washington, DC just recently and they were selling these at $20 each and I thought what a

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:03.230
great idea that was.  Did I needed it?  No. But did I pay the $20? I sure did.  So I

00:21:03.230 --> 00:21:06.440
really liked their initiative and we
started doing this in the countries I

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:10.780
went to in South America.  And the nice
thing is that the Center for Disease

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:15.200
Control has actually made this really
easy for you.  You make this kit.  You put in

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:18.680
a bag.  You sell it for twenty dollars.  And
they've even

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:22.910
printed information that you can copy,
download from the website,

00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:27.110
print out, and hand out to the
families that you sold the kits to,

00:21:27.110 --> 00:21:31.920
and it tells them how to use the kit.  So all
the information you need is there.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:36.660
And again, I have two children.  They're
both in middle school and this is what

00:21:36.670 --> 00:21:40.490
they are going to do in the summer break - focus on selling Zika kits.

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:45.560
So let's look
at the mosquito life cycle, and this is

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:49.410
really important to understanding the
risk of transmission of Zika.

00:21:50.220 --> 00:21:55.340
We know it's not the male mosquito; it's the
female mosquito that bites us.  So we only get

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:56.950
bitten by female mosquitoes.

00:21:57.580 --> 00:22:02.920
They lay eggs in water and they,
what's really remarkable about these Aedes

00:22:02.929 --> 00:22:07.080
mosquitoes, they can lay their eggs in a
very small amount of water, and the eggs

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:11.880
can either hatch or they can survive for many, many months.  And we know here in Washington, DC

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:15.460
that we have mosquitoes that lay their
eggs, they survive the winter, they

00:22:15.460 --> 00:22:18.960
survive the snow, and it's still there
in the spring and the summer and they hatch out.

00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:25.920
Usually these eggs though, they hatch, you know, in a few days in a few days to a few months,

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:30.160
and they produce what's called
larvae.  Those larvae, within 5 days,

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:35.060
go to pupae, and those pupae then live in the water and the develop into young

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:37.640
flying mosquitoes in 2 to 3 days.

00:22:37.660 --> 00:22:42.040
And then takes a few weeks for those mosquitoes, those

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:46.220
flying mosquitoes, then to mature where they can actually transmitted the virus.

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:50.600
But if you understand the mosquito life cycle, then you also understand

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:54.240
the second opportunity I want to give high school students, and things that you can do.

00:22:55.580 --> 00:22:59.420
And one of those you can do is help the local county health department,

00:22:59.420 --> 00:23:04.380
or the local mosquito vector
control program, in identifying

00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:08.400
where there are eggs, where there are a larvae, where there are pupae, where there are young

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:12.230
adult mosquitoes hatching in water, and
where there is actually mosquitoes with white

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:16.550
bands on the legs actually biting people.  And you can see the five photos here

00:23:16.550 --> 00:23:18.470
show you those life cycles.

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:24.300
And so what we've done throughout community

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:29.390
engagement, through using high school
students, we've actually started programs

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:33.210
where they can go out and help the
community.  And it's really important

00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:37.350
because we know how to control
mosquitoes.  We're just not really good at

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:41.270
it, because we don't take, we don't pay much
attention.  But this year we've really

00:23:41.270 --> 00:23:45.820
got to focus on how to control mosquitoes
because we have this risk of this, of this

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:49.980
Zika virus.  And we know that pot
plants are going to have water.  We know that

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:54.610
there are swimming pools.  We know there are areas that, like car tires, that

00:23:54.610 --> 00:23:58.679
contain water where the mosquitoes can
breed. We know that if we see any water,

00:23:58.679 --> 00:24:02.070
we have to drain it and tip it out and
get rid of it.  We know that we put

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:05.750
flowers in vases.  Over a very short
period of time,

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:09.480
mosquitos could lay their eggs there.  We
know that any barrels,

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:12.900
containers, buckets that we have in the back yard or front yard that have water, we have

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:17.320
to put a lid on top of them.  And we also
know that we have to protect our homes.

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:21.620
Whether we've got air conditioning or we don't have air conditioning, how do we put insect

00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:26.130
screens on the wall, on the on
the windows, on the doors, and keep them

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:28.930
closed to ensure the mosquitoes don't
come inside?

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:33.700
So here in the US,

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:39.420
at every county, at every city, in every town, there are mosquito surveillance programs on

00:24:39.429 --> 00:24:44.510
going right now, and they can really
use your help.  And from the five

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:47.809
countries I went to in South America, they had mosquito surveillance programs and we

00:24:47.809 --> 00:24:52.089
engage the students from the high schools
down there to actually help those programs.

00:24:52.089 --> 00:24:55.720
And what these mosquitoes
surveillance programs do, they estimate

00:24:55.720 --> 00:24:59.500
the number of mosquitoes, the mosquito
abundance in the area.  Remember those mosquitoes

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:03.160
only fly a hundred and fifty feet, or
about the length of a football field

00:25:03.160 --> 00:25:08.860
from where they actually breed, so they
don't fly miles.  We use the estimates by

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:14.040
counting the mosquitoes in their life
cycle - eggs, pupae, larvae, and adults- and we

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:17.800
indicate the levels of risk at the
local neighborhood level.

00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:23.720
And then we do this maybe every day, every week, so we can compare the results over time and location.

00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:24.880
We put them on maps.

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:29.679
So, again, you could take a real important role

00:25:29.679 --> 00:25:35.559
yourself in your community and where
you live by helping your local county

00:25:35.559 --> 00:25:39.460
health department, or your local mosquito
vector control program, by doing larvae

00:25:39.460 --> 00:25:45.090
and pupea surveys, by this little
traps that we can get that trap catch

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:49.890
eggs and we can also get sticky traps
that catch adult mosquitoes, and

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:54.110
we can identify do they have what bands
on their legs and, if they do, we report them.

00:25:56.000 --> 00:25:57.980
And what do we do with that information?

00:25:57.980 --> 00:26:03.919
Well, we've had mosquito diseases that
have been transferred by mosquito.  We've had them

00:26:03.919 --> 00:26:07.890
for years here in the US.  We had West Nile
virus.  And here's a map of New York

00:26:07.890 --> 00:26:12.990
where, not just the government, but also
the schools in New York, helped set out some

00:26:12.990 --> 00:26:18.450
traps and help send in information every
day.  And the way they did that, they used

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:22.350
their cellphones. I'm going to show you how we use cell phones to send in the

00:26:22.350 --> 00:26:25.419
critical, that really important
information, that's needed.  And this is a

00:26:25.419 --> 00:26:29.410
map of New York  which showed
with the traps were laid out for

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:35.250
West Nile virus.  And they took all that
information every day, and they produced

00:26:35.250 --> 00:26:41.050
these heat maps. These maps are
risk maps, and you can see the darker the color,

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:45.720
the higher the concentration or
the higher the number of mosquitoes,

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:52.169
so therefore, the higher risk.  And places like New York City, New York State, places like

00:26:52.169 --> 00:26:58.949
Florida, Georgia, Alabama, California, Hawaii are producing these maps every day to show us the risk.

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:06.620
So again, you could participate and help identify mosquitoes on a daily basis,

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:09.300
and then call in to the county health department

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:11.500
and let them know the result of what you find.

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:16.680
The other project, the third project, we set up in South America with high school students

00:27:16.680 --> 00:27:20.520
was that we know how to kill mosquitoes, we use insecticides,

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:23.340
but the big problem we have that sometimes over

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:29.100
time we use the same insecticides over and over again, we get what's called resistance,

00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:34.640
and mosquitoes who develop resistance
don't die that well form using insecticides.

00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:37.460
So one of the projects we
set up was with students was taking a

00:27:37.470 --> 00:27:44.940
glass jar, capturing mosquitoes.  You
put the insecticide around the glass jar,

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:49.340
catch the mosquitoes, and you leave them there for an hour and you count how many of those mosquitos died.

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:54.020
If they die, then we show that the mosquito insecticide is working.

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:56.960
If they didn't die, then we got to
call the county health department say -

00:27:56.960 --> 00:28:00.299
"Hey, look at this; the mosquitoes didn't die" - and we're going to work out why.  That's

00:28:00.299 --> 00:28:04.470
information we can find.  It doesn't
cost much money to get a glass jar and

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:07.050
conduct these experiments, so it's a really
good science experiment.

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:12.799
The Center for Disease Control has actually published the protocols, the procedures, of how to

00:28:12.800 --> 00:28:16.360
set up these experiments.  And this is a
picture taken from the manual that's available

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:19.420
on the website, available online, to do
this.

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:25.000
And I want to give you a real world example now.  This is Orange County in California.

00:28:25.540 --> 00:28:27.420
This is their vector control program.

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:30.560
And taken from their website this morning, they have a big sign up

00:28:30.560 --> 00:28:31.960
"We need your help!"

00:28:32.860 --> 00:28:35.420
And they want every
citizen, they want

00:28:35.450 --> 00:28:39.750
every person, throughout the whole
neighborhood, throughout the community, to

00:28:39.750 --> 00:28:43.840
report that, if they get bitten or they see
mosquitoes biting you in the daytime,

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:49.340
to ring this number.  That's a really
good idea and we're seeing counties and

00:28:49.340 --> 00:28:53.320
towns and cities throughout the country do this and setup these programs.

00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:56.780
But the thing is high school students,

00:28:56.780 --> 00:28:58.780
a lot of you have these, the phones.

00:28:59.460 --> 00:29:03.120
So why do we, why do we want to make a phone call?  Why don't we use an app?

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:07.039
Why don't we send a text message? Why don't we use social media?  Why don't we use

00:29:07.039 --> 00:29:12.959
Facebook or Twitter or WhatsApp?
And this is really important for Ebola disease.

00:29:12.960 --> 00:29:16.760
For the nurses to communicate, we used WhatsApp.  What we set up now for

00:29:16.760 --> 00:29:20.950
students is an actual using an app
to actually report.  So you don't actually have to

00:29:20.950 --> 00:29:23.700
make a phone call and talk to a person.  You can actually just

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:28.780
record it on your phone like a text message and send it in to their surveillance system.

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:32.740
And this is what
we've done, and for the app you can

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:37.899
download and you can put in on your phone looks something like this.  It'll have your name, has your

00:29:37.899 --> 00:29:39.739
address, has your phone number

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:45.640
tells you, well, probably at my house, are there dogs on the property because that's really important.

00:29:45.640 --> 00:29:48.240
When the mosquito control people
come out, they want to know whether these

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:51.880
dogs are around in your yard so they don't get bitten.

00:29:52.400 --> 00:29:55.600
"Where did you see the mosquito?"  "Oh, we saw them around the oak trees."

00:29:56.160 --> 00:30:00.320
Maybe you might got a photo of it.  If you got a photo, you can send the photo on the app.

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:08.060
And it's really important that we know the
location of where you are when you send in your report.

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:12.580
And many of us don't have
to try very hard to send in the location,

00:30:12.590 --> 00:30:17.679
because this is a really cool app, and I want you to Google this.  Whenever I get home work to my

00:30:17.679 --> 00:30:19.909
my son and my daughter who are in middle
school

00:30:19.909 --> 00:30:23.309
and I try to tell them let's do your homework, they go "Dad can't we just Google it?"

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:30.780
And so what you can do, if you can Google "What Three Words" and What Three Words is on the website,

00:30:30.789 --> 00:30:36.409
has a website.  It also has an app.
What Three Word has taken the world

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:41.900
and  has divided the world up into squares that are 9 feet by 9 feet and they've put

00:30:41.909 --> 00:30:47.159
three words for every square.  Now to cover the
whole globe

00:30:47.159 --> 00:30:49.799
it takes fifty seven trillion squares.

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:55.920
So they have taken fifty seven trillion squares 
9 feet by 9 feet,

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:59.940
given them a three-word
identification, and you can now use their app

00:30:59.940 --> 00:31:03.320
to identify where you are when you
see a mosquito, or when you see an egg,

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:09.020
or when you see pupae or larvae, or something else
you want to report.  If you send me those

00:31:09.020 --> 00:31:13.600
three words to my phone I would then be
out to identify exactly where you are,

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:18.340
where your square is, 9 feet by 9 feet.
And we use this all the time.

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:25.320
It's a really easy way for us to put
information on a map and it's called What Three Words.

00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:28.340
So in Orange County

00:31:28.780 --> 00:31:32.900
this is what they are doing as people either
call in information or send in information.

00:31:32.900 --> 00:31:37.760
Everyday they are making maps.  And this is
one of the maps and, as you can see, the

00:31:37.760 --> 00:31:42.320
red areas is where this Aedes aegypti
 mosquitoes are being found and the yellow

00:31:42.320 --> 00:31:46.540
areas is where Aedes albopictus
mosquitoes are being found.  And they put this on the

00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:50.420
Internet, on the website, so that every
person in the community can check this

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:54.400
website and they know where the
mosquitoes have been found the previous day.

00:31:57.640 --> 00:32:00.440
So I've given you a couple of projects
that high school students can do.  Ive given you a

00:32:00.440 --> 00:32:03.120
couple of ways that you can make some
money over the summer.

00:32:03.140 --> 00:32:08.540
I just want to talk about - there are lots of things that we know, but they're still many more things

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:11.590
that we don't know about this Zika virus, and we're learning every day.

00:32:12.160 --> 00:32:14.760
So just to summarize what we know.

00:32:14.760 --> 00:32:21.660
Zika  can be spread from a pregnant woman to her unborn baby, the fetus, during pregnancy, we know that.

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:26.620
We now know, as of April 13,
this is only very new scientific

00:32:26.620 --> 00:32:32.060
information, that the infection in
pregnancy can be linked to birth defects.

00:32:32.060 --> 00:32:33.820
But there's still a lot to learn about
that.

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:39.720
We know that the Zika is spread mostly by the bite of an infected Aedes species mosquito.

00:32:39.720 --> 00:32:43.340
These are these mosquitoes with white bands on their legs.

00:32:43.340 --> 00:32:47.700
And even though these mosquitoes mainly bite in the day, they can also bite at night time.

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:51.460
We also know today

00:32:52.280 --> 00:33:00.140
that the Zika virus is not locally transmitted by mosquitoes in the US 48 continental states,

00:33:00.140 --> 00:33:01.960
it's not in Alaska, it's not in Hawaii either.

00:33:02.500 --> 00:33:08.120
But we do know those mosquitos are found here.  And because these mosquitoes

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:12.520
that spread ,or can spread Zika, are found through many countries throughout the world,

00:33:12.520 --> 00:33:16.220
we're going to see many more countries experience Zika outbreaks.

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:22.000
The last thing that we know is that there is no vaccine for Zika and there's no

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:26.380
medicine or treatment for Zika.  We have to
treat the symptoms,

00:33:26.380 --> 00:33:31.540
but there's no actual cure for Zika, and
that's really important to understand.

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:37.220
A couple of things that we don't know and
we'll research on that scientists are

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:42.620
working on right now today.  When is there
